  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 135 PM CDT Thu Oct 15 2026\n\nThis afternoon, a cold front is moving through eastern KS ahead of a \nlead trough axis aloft. Northwest winds will increase behind the \nfront as CAA brings in a cooler and drier air mass from the north. \nTemperatures are likely to drop this afternoon given the strong CAA \npattern. Hi-res models then show an increase in northwest winds for a\nbrief period late this evening along a secondary boundary ahead of a\nstronger trough axis moving in from the Northern Plains. \nTemperatures fall into the low 40s tonight with gusty winds and \nclearing skies. A stratus cloud deck then looks to move back over the\narea tomorrow from the northeast. Continued CAA with the approach of\na surface ridge axis and low cloud cover will make for a chilly day \nwith highs only reaching the low to mid 50s tomorrow afternoon.\n\nThe downward trend in temperatures continues into Saturday morning \nas a nearly 1040 mb ridge axis settles right overhead. Confidence \nhas been increasing in temperatres falling to near freezing for a \ngood portion of the area with models agreeing on the favorable \nsetup. Thus, in coordination with neighboring offices, a Freeze \nWatch has been issued for the northwestern half of the forecast area \nFriday night and early Saturday morning. The rest of the area will \nlikely see headlines as well with area-wide frost looking likely. \nFrom there, a gradual warming trend is expected as the ridge quickly \nmoves east, bringing southerly flow back across the region. High \ntemperatures Saturday afternoon should approach 60 degrees, then \ntrend about five degrees warmer each afternoon into the start of \nnext week.\n\nThe synoptic pattern across the CONUS shifts to a broad area of \nsouthwest flow aloft from the southwestern US into the Midwest early \nin the workweek. Long range deterministic models show periodic \nshortwaves riding through the flow, however, limited forcing and \nmoisture availability should keep POPs low through Tuesday, then \nslightly higher probabilities midweek as moisture increases. Rain \nand storm chances continue to increase later in the week with models\nin fairly good agreement showing a deeper trough axis approaching \nthe area from the west.",
